{"data":[{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16618","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16618","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.16618","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pengfei","familyName":"Hao","name":"Hao, Pengfei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongqiu","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Hongqiu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuaibo","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Shuaibo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhaohu","familyName":"Xing","name":"Xing, Zhaohu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guang","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Guang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kaishun","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Kaishun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lei","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Lei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Surgical-MambaLLM: Mamba2-enhanced Multimodal Large Language Model for VQLA in Robotic Surgery"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-20T10:42:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T00:26:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In recent years, Visual Question Localized-Answering in robotic surgery (Surgical-VQLA) has gained significant attention for its potential to assist medical students and junior doctors in understanding surgical scenes. Recently, the rapid development of Large Language Models (LLMs) has provided more promising solutions for this task. However, current methods struggle to establish complex dependencies between text and visual details, and have difficulty perceiving the spatial information of surgical scenes. To address these challenges, we propose a novel method, Surgical-MambaLLM, which is the first to combine Mamba2 with LLM in the surgical domain, that leverages Mamba2's ability to effectively capture cross-modal dependencies and perceive spatial information in surgical scenes, thereby enhancing the LLMs' understanding of surgical images. Specifically, we propose the Cross-modal Bidirectional Mamba2 Integration (CBMI) module to leverage Mamba2 for effective multimodal fusion, with its cross-modal integration capabilities. Additionally, tailored to the geometric characteristics of surgical scenes, we design the Surgical Instrument Perception (SIP) scanning mode for Mamba2 to scan the surgical images, enhancing the model's spatial understanding of the surgical scene. Extensive experiments demonstrate that our Surgical-MambaLLM model outperforms the state-of-the-art methods on the EndoVis17-VQLA and EndoVis18-VQLA datasets, significantly improving the performance of the Surgical-VQLA task."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Early accepted by MICCAI2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.16618","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-23T02:52:37Z","registered":"2025-09-23T02:52:37Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-23T02:52:37Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16617","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16617","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.16617","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David","familyName":"Kreismann","name":"Kreismann, David","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Detection and Simulation of Urban Heat Islands Using a Fine-Tuned Geospatial Foundation Model"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.6; I.5.4; I.6.8","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-20T10:41:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T00:26:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"As urbanization and climate change progress, urban heat island effects are becoming more frequent and severe. To formulate effective mitigation plans, cities require detailed air temperature data. However, predictive analytics methods based on conventional machine learning models and limited data infrastructure often provide inaccurate predictions, especially in underserved areas. In this context, geospatial foundation models trained on unstructured global data demonstrate strong generalization and require minimal fine-tuning, offering an alternative for predictions where traditional approaches are limited. This study fine-tunes a geospatial foundation model to predict urban land surface temperatures under future climate scenarios and explores its response to land cover changes using simulated vegetation strategies. The fine-tuned model achieved pixel-wise downscaling errors below 1.74 °C and aligned with ground truth patterns, demonstrating an extrapolation capacity up to 3.62 °C."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"12 pages, 4 figures, to appear in GI LNI (SKILL 2025)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.16617","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-23T02:52:35Z","registered":"2025-09-23T02:52:36Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-23T02:52:36Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16611","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16611","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.16611","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiwei","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Xiwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiwei","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yiwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yansong","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Yansong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fan","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Fan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Teng","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Teng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhonghua","familyName":"Miao","name":"Miao, Zhonghua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sami","familyName":"Haddadin","name":"Haddadin, Sami","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alois","familyName":"Knoll","name":"Knoll, Alois","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Video-to-BT: Generating Reactive Behavior Trees from Human Demonstration Videos for Robotic Assembly"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-20T10:21:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T00:25:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Modern manufacturing demands robotic assembly systems with enhanced flexibility and reliability. However, traditional approaches often rely on programming tailored to each product by experts for fixed settings, which are inherently inflexible to product changes and lack the robustness to handle variations. As Behavior Trees (BTs) are increasingly used in robotics for their modularity and reactivity, we propose a novel hierarchical framework, Video-to-BT, that seamlessly integrates high-level cognitive planning with low-level reactive control, with BTs serving both as the structured output of planning and as the governing structure for execution. Our approach leverages a Vision-Language Model (VLM) to decompose human demonstration videos into subtasks, from which Behavior Trees are generated. During the execution, the planned BTs combined with real-time scene interpretation enable the system to operate reactively in the dynamic environment, while VLM-driven replanning is triggered upon execution failure. This closed-loop architecture ensures stability and adaptivity. We validate our framework on real-world assembly tasks through a series of experiments, demonstrating high planning reliability, robust performance in long-horizon assembly tasks, and strong generalization across diverse and perturbed conditions. Project website: https://video2bt.github.io/video2bt_page/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.16611","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-23T02:52:27Z","registered":"2025-09-23T02:52:28Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-23T02:52:28Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16610","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16610","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.16610","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junhao","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Junhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingbo","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Jingbo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiang","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Xiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haidong","familyName":"Xin","name":"Xin, Haidong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuhao","familyName":"Xue","name":"Xue, Yuhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yibin","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Yibin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"LLMsPark: A Benchmark for Evaluating Large Language Models in Strategic Gaming Contexts"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-20T10:21:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T00:25:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"As large language models (LLMs) advance across diverse tasks, the need for comprehensive evaluation beyond single metrics becomes increasingly important. To fully assess LLM intelligence, it is crucial to examine their interactive dynamics and strategic behaviors. We present LLMsPark, a game theory-based evaluation platform that measures LLMs' decision-making strategies and social behaviors in classic game-theoretic settings, providing a multi-agent environment to explore strategic depth. Our system cross-evaluates 15 leading LLMs (both commercial and open-source) using leaderboard rankings and scoring mechanisms. Higher scores reflect stronger reasoning and strategic capabilities, revealing distinct behavioral patterns and performance differences across models. This work introduces a novel perspective for evaluating LLMs' strategic intelligence, enriching existing benchmarks and broadening their assessment in interactive, game-theoretic scenarios. The benchmark and rankings are publicly available at https://llmsparks.github.io/."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by EMNLP 2025 Findings"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.16610","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-23T02:52:25Z","registered":"2025-09-23T02:52:26Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-23T02:52:26Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16603","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16603","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.16603","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Maurício do V. M.","familyName":"da Costa","name":"da Costa, Maurício do V. M.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eloi","familyName":"Moliner","name":"Moliner, Eloi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"An Octave-based Multi-Resolution CQT Architecture for Diffusion-based Audio Generation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Audio and Speech Processing (eess.AS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Sound (cs.SD)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-20T09:57:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T00:25:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This paper introduces MR-CQTdiff, a novel neural-network architecture for diffusion-based audio generation that leverages a multi-resolution Constant-$Q$ Transform (C$Q$T). The proposed architecture employs an efficient, invertible CQT framework that adjusts the time-frequency resolution on an octave-by-octave basis. This design addresses the issue of low temporal resolution at lower frequencies, enabling more flexible and expressive audio generation. We conduct an evaluation using the Fréchet Audio Distance (FAD) metric across various architectures and two datasets. Experimental results demonstrate that MR-CQTdiff achieves state-of-the-art audio quality, outperforming competing architectures."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"accepted at IEEE International Symposium on the Internet of Sounds"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.16603","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-23T02:52:15Z","registered":"2025-09-23T02:52:16Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-23T02:52:16Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16597","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16597","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.16597","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Luyan","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Luyan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MCP: A Control-Theoretic Orchestration Framework for Synergistic Efficiency and Interpretability in Multimodal Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.7; I.2.6","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-20T09:44:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T00:24:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Aiming at the problems of computational inefficiency and insufficient interpretability faced by large models in complex tasks such as multi-round reasoning and multi-modal collaboration, this study proposes a three-layer collaboration framework based on model-controller-task adaptation (MCP). By decoupling large model functions into reasoning, generation and retrieval modules, and combining reinforcement learning-driven dynamic routing algorithms and task adaptation mechanisms, the systematic integration of control theory and large model dynamic reasoning is achieved for the first time. Experiments show that the MCP framework improves the performance of cross-modal benchmarking tasks, such as GLUE, COCO, ScienceQA, etc., by 15-30% compared with the baseline model, improves the reasoning efficiency by 40%, and generates the interpretable intermediate results through the Presenter layer, obtaining 90% of the manual interpretability scores, which provides a brand-new technological path to solve the bottleneck of the practical application of the large model."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"13 pages, 6 figures, 2 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.16597","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-23T02:52:07Z","registered":"2025-09-23T02:52:08Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-23T02:52:08Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16590","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16590","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.16590","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Manuel","familyName":"Borroto","name":"Borroto, Manuel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Katie","familyName":"Gallagher","name":"Gallagher, Katie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Antonio","familyName":"Ielo","name":"Ielo, Antonio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Irfan","familyName":"Kareem","name":"Kareem, Irfan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Francesco","familyName":"Ricca","name":"Ricca, Francesco","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alessandra","familyName":"Russo","name":"Russo, Alessandra","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Question Answering with LLMs and Learning from Answer Sets"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Logic in Computer Science (cs.LO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-20T09:26:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T00:24:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) excel at understanding natural language but struggle with explicit commonsense reasoning. A recent trend of research suggests that the combination of LLM with robust symbolic reasoning systems can overcome this problem on story-based question answering tasks. In this setting, existing approaches typically depend on human expertise to manually craft the symbolic component. We argue, however, that this component can also be automatically learned from examples. In this work, we introduce LLM2LAS, a hybrid system that effectively combines the natural language understanding capabilities of LLMs, the rule induction power of the Learning from Answer Sets (LAS) system ILASP, and the formal reasoning strengths of Answer Set Programming (ASP). LLMs are used to extract semantic structures from text, which ILASP then transforms into interpretable logic rules. These rules allow an ASP solver to perform precise and consistent reasoning, enabling correct answers to previously unseen questions. Empirical results outline the strengths and weaknesses of our automatic approach for learning and reasoning in a story-based question answering benchmark."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Under consideration for TPLP journal"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.16590","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-23T02:51:57Z","registered":"2025-09-23T02:51:58Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-23T02:51:58Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16578","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16578","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.16578","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenyao","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Wenyao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ran","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Ran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pengyang","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Pengyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuanchun","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Yuanchun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pengfei","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Pengfei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Zero-Shot Human Mobility Forecasting via Large Language Model with Hierarchical Reasoning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-20T08:46:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T00:23:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Human mobility forecasting is important for applications such as transportation planning, urban management, and personalized recommendations. However, existing methods often fail to generalize to unseen users or locations and struggle to capture dynamic intent due to limited labeled data and the complexity of mobility patterns. We propose ZHMF, a framework for zero-shot human mobility forecasting that combines a semantic enhanced retrieval and reflection mechanism with a hierarchical language model based reasoning system. The task is reformulated as a natural language question answering paradigm. Leveraging LLMs semantic understanding of user histories and context, our approach handles previously unseen prediction scenarios. We further introduce a hierarchical reflection mechanism for iterative reasoning and refinement by decomposing forecasting into an activity level planner and a location level selector, enabling collaborative modeling of long term user intentions and short term contextual preferences. Experiments on standard human mobility datasets show that our approach outperforms existing models. Ablation studies reveal the contribution of each module, and case studies illustrate how the method captures user intentions and adapts to diverse contextual scenarios."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.16578","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-23T02:51:40Z","registered":"2025-09-23T02:51:41Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-23T02:51:41Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16571","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16571","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.16571","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"R.","familyName":"He","name":"He, R.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Enhancing Antimicrobial Molecule Prediction via Dynamic Routing Capsule Networks and Multi-Source Molecular Embeddings"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Quantitative Methods (q-bio.QM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-20T08:09:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T00:21:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Antibiotics are a vital class of drugs closely associated with the prevention and treatment of bacterial infections. Accurate prediction of molecular antimicrobial activity remains a key challenge in the pursuit of novel antibiotic candidates. However, laboratory-based antimicrobial compounds identification is costly, time-consuming, and prone to rediscovering known antibiotics, highlighting the urgent need for efficient and accurate computational models. Recent advances in machine learning (ML) and deep learning (DL) have significantly enhanced the ability to explore chemical space and identify potential antimicrobial compounds. In this study, we particularly emphasize deep learning models and employ five chemistry language models tailored for chemical data to encode small molecules. Our model incorporates a unique capsule network architecture and introduces innovations in loss function selection and feature processing modules, demonstrating superior performance in predicting inhibitory activities against Escherichia coli and Acinetobacter baumannii. We conducted a series of ablation studies to elucidate the contributions of network design and input features. Case studies validated the usability and effectiveness of our model.To facilitate accessibility, we developed an intuitive web portal to disseminate this novel tool. Our results indicate that the proposed approach offers improved predictive accuracy and enhanced interpretability, underscoring the potential of interpretable artificial intelligence methods in accelerating antibiotic discovery and addressing the urgent challenge of antimicrobial resistance."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.16571","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-23T02:51:31Z","registered":"2025-09-23T02:51:31Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-23T02:51:31Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16564","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16564","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.16564","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jun Rong Brian","familyName":"Chong","name":"Chong, Jun Rong Brian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yixuan","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Yixuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anthony K. H.","familyName":"Tung","name":"Tung, Anthony K. H.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MPCG: Multi-Round Persona-Conditioned Generation for Modeling the Evolution of Misinformation with LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Social and Information Networks (cs.SI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-20T07:40:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T00:21:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Misinformation evolves as it spreads, shifting in language, framing, and moral emphasis to adapt to new audiences. However, current misinformation detection approaches implicitly assume that misinformation is static. We introduce MPCG, a multi-round, persona-conditioned framework that simulates how claims are iteratively reinterpreted by agents with distinct ideological perspectives. Our approach uses an uncensored large language model (LLM) to generate persona-specific claims across multiple rounds, conditioning each generation on outputs from the previous round, enabling the study of misinformation evolution. We evaluate the generated claims through human and LLM-based annotations, cognitive effort metrics (readability, perplexity), emotion evocation metrics (sentiment analysis, morality), clustering, feasibility, and downstream classification. Results show strong agreement between human and GPT-4o-mini annotations, with higher divergence in fluency judgments. Generated claims require greater cognitive effort than the original claims and consistently reflect persona-aligned emotional and moral framing. Clustering and cosine similarity analyses confirm semantic drift across rounds while preserving topical coherence. Feasibility results show a 77% feasibility rate, confirming suitability for downstream tasks. Classification results reveal that commonly used misinformation detectors experience macro-F1 performance drops of up to 49.7%. The code is available at https://github.com/bcjr1997/MPCG"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"35 pages, 8 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.16564","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-23T02:51:21Z","registered":"2025-09-23T02:51:22Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-23T02:51:22Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16561","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16561","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.16561","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yue","familyName":"Xin","name":"Xin, Yue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chen","familyName":"Shen","name":"Shen, Chen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shaotian","familyName":"Yan","name":"Yan, Shaotian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaosong","familyName":"Yuan","name":"Yuan, Xiaosong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yaoming","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yaoming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaofeng","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Xiaofeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenxi","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Chenxi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jieping","familyName":"Ye","name":"Ye, Jieping","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SalaMAnder: Shapley-based Mathematical Expression Attribution and Metric for Chain-of-Thought Reasoning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-20T07:38:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T00:21:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Chain-of-Thought (CoT) prompting enhances the math reasoning capability of large language models (LLMs) to a large margin. However, the mechanism underlying such improvements remains unexplored. In this paper, we present \\textbf{SalaMAnder} (\\textbf{S}h\\textbf{a}p\\textbf{l}ey-b\\textbf{a}sed \\textbf{M}athematical Expression \\textbf{A}ttribution a\\textbf{nd} M\\textbf{e}t\\textbf{r}ic), a theoretically grounded methodology as well as a mathematically rigorous evaluation metric for quantifying component-level contributions in few-shot CoT reasoning. Concretely, we leverage the Shapley value for mathematical expression attribution and develop an efficient stratified sampling algorithm that significantly reduces the computational complexity. Besides, we develop the \\textbf{CoSP} (\\textbf{C}ardinality \\textbf{o}f \\textbf{S}hapley \\textbf{P}ositives) metric through covariance analysis. Comprehensive validation across popular LLM models and diverse mathematical benchmarks demonstrates that the CoSP metric within our SalaMAnder framework exhibits a robust monotonic correlation with model performance, not only providing theoretical explanations for the empirical success of existing few-shot CoT but also establishing mathematically rigorous principles for prompt construction optimization. Furthermore, we verify the reliability of the explanation, based on which we unify the insights of previous work."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"accpeted by EMNLP 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.16561","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-23T02:51:17Z","registered":"2025-09-23T02:51:18Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-23T02:51:18Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16560","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16560","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.16560","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ji Soo","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Ji Soo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Byungoh","familyName":"Ko","name":"Ko, Byungoh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jaewon","familyName":"Cho","name":"Cho, Jaewon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Howoong","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Howoong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jaewoon","familyName":"Byun","name":"Byun, Jaewoon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hyunwoo J.","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Hyunwoo J.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Captioning for Text-Video Retrieval via Dual-Group Direct Preference Optimization"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-20T07:36:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T00:20:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In text-video retrieval, auxiliary captions are often used to enhance video understanding, bridging the gap between the modalities. While recent advances in multi-modal large language models (MLLMs) have enabled strong zero-shot caption generation, we observe that such captions tend to be generic and indistinguishable across visually similar videos, limiting their utility for fine-grained retrieval. Moreover, conventional captioning approaches are typically evaluated using language generation metrics, such as BLEU, which are not typically tailored for retrieval tasks that require making discriminative distinctions between candidates. To address this, we propose $\\textbf{CaRe-DPO}$, a retrieval framework that directly optimizes caption generation using retrieval relevance scores. At its core is Dual-Group Direct Preference Optimization (DG-DPO), a novel learning strategy that supervises captioning by modeling preferences across groups of distinct video and caption pairs. In addition, we present an MLLM-based retrieval model that incorporates role-embeddings to better distinguish between textual inputs with different functional roles, such as an auxiliary caption and a text query. Through extensive experiments, we demonstrate that CaRe-DPO significantly enhances retrieval performance by effectively leveraging auxiliary knowledge to generate fine-grained captions for retrieval. Code is available at https://github.com/mlvlab/CaReDPO."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"EMNLP 2025 Findings"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.16560","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-23T02:51:16Z","registered":"2025-09-23T02:51:16Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-23T02:51:16Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16558","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16558","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.16558","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingjian","familyName":"Duan","name":"Duan, Mingjian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ming","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Ming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shenghao","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Shenghao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaheng","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Jiaheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weili","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Weili","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MoPE: A Mixture of Password Experts for Improving Password Guessing"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-20T07:30:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T00:20:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Textual passwords remain a predominant authentication mechanism in web security. To evaluate their strength, existing research has proposed several data-driven models across various scenarios. However, these models generally treat passwords uniformly, neglecting the structural differences among passwords. This typically results in biased training that favors frequent password structural patterns. To mitigate the biased training, we argue that passwords, as a type of complex short textual data, should be processed in a structure-aware manner by identifying their structural patterns and routing them to specialized models accordingly. In this paper, we propose MoPE, a Mixture of Password Experts framework, specifically designed to leverage the structural patterns in passwords to improveguessing performance. Motivated by the observation that passwords with similar structural patterns (e.g., fixed-length numeric strings) tend to cluster in high-density regions within the latent space, our MoPE introduces: (1) a novel structure-based method for generating specialized expert models; (2) a lightweight gate method to select appropriate expert models to output reliable guesses, better aligned with the high computational frequency of password guessing tasks. Our evaluation shows that MoPE significantly outperforms existing state-of-the-art baselines in both offline and online guessing scenarios, achieving up to 38.80% and 9.27% improvement in cracking rate, respectively, showcasing that MoPE can effectively exploit the capabilities of data-driven models for password guessing. Additionally, we implement a real-time Password Strength Meter (PSM) based on offline MoPE, assisting users in choosing stronger passwords more precisely with millisecond-level response latency."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.16558","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-23T02:51:13Z","registered":"2025-09-23T02:51:13Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-23T02:51:13Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16543","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16543","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.16543","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yue","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Yue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhengzhe","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Zhengzhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaonan","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Xiaonan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kehan","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Kehan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haomin","familyName":"Zhuang","name":"Zhuang, Haomin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yujun","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Yujun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhengqing","familyName":"Yuan","name":"Yuan, Zhengqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoqi","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Xiaoqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jules","familyName":"Schleinitz","name":"Schleinitz, Jules","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanbo","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yanbo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuhao","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Shuhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mihir","familyName":"Surve","name":"Surve, Mihir","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nitesh V","familyName":"Chawla","name":"Chawla, Nitesh V","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Olaf","familyName":"Wiest","name":"Wiest, Olaf","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiangliang","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Xiangliang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ChemOrch: Empowering LLMs with Chemical Intelligence via Synthetic Instructions"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-20T05:43:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T00:18:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Empowering large language models (LLMs) with chemical intelligence remains a challenge due to the scarcity of high-quality, domain-specific instruction-response datasets and the misalignment of existing synthetic data generation pipelines with the inherently hierarchical and rule-governed structure of chemical information. To address this, we propose ChemOrch, a framework that synthesizes chemically grounded instruction-response pairs through a two-stage process: task-controlled instruction generation and tool-aware response construction. ChemOrch enables controllable diversity and levels of difficulty for the generated tasks, and ensures response precision through tool planning and distillation, and tool-based self-repair mechanisms. The effectiveness of ChemOrch is evaluated based on: 1) the high quality of generated instruction data, demonstrating superior diversity and strong alignment with chemical constraints; 2) the reliable generation of evaluation tasks that more effectively reveal LLM weaknesses in chemistry; and 3) the significant improvement of LLM chemistry capabilities when the generated instruction data are used for fine-tuning. Our work thus represents a critical step toward scalable and verifiable chemical intelligence in LLMs."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.16543","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-23T02:50:52Z","registered":"2025-09-23T02:50:53Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-23T02:50:53Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16542","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16542","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.16542","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Khalid","familyName":"Hasan","name":"Hasan, Khalid","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jamil","familyName":"Saquer","name":"Saquer, Jamil","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yifan","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yifan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Mental Multi-class Classification on Social Media: Benchmarking Transformer Architectures against LSTM Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-20T05:41:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T00:18:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Millions of people openly share mental health struggles on social media, providing rich data for early detection of conditions such as depression, bipolar disorder, etc. However, most prior Natural Language Processing (NLP) research has focused on single-disorder identification, leaving a gap in understanding the efficacy of advanced NLP techniques for distinguishing among multiple mental health conditions. In this work, we present a large-scale comparative study of state-of-the-art transformer versus Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM)-based models to classify mental health posts into exclusive categories of mental health conditions. We first curate a large dataset of Reddit posts spanning six mental health conditions and a control group, using rigorous filtering and statistical exploratory analysis to ensure annotation quality. We then evaluate five transformer architectures (BERT, RoBERTa, DistilBERT, ALBERT, and ELECTRA) against several LSTM variants (with or without attention, using contextual or static embeddings) under identical conditions. Experimental results show that transformer models consistently outperform the alternatives, with RoBERTa achieving 91-99% F1-scores and accuracies across all classes. Notably, attention-augmented LSTMs with BERT embeddings approach transformer performance (up to 97% F1-score) while training 2-3.5 times faster, whereas LSTMs using static embeddings fail to learn useful signals. These findings represent the first comprehensive benchmark for multi-class mental health detection, offering practical guidance on model selection and highlighting an accuracy-efficiency trade-off for real-world deployment of mental health NLP systems."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"24th IEEE International Conference on Machine Learning and Applications, ICMLA 2025 (camera-ready)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.16542","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-23T02:50:51Z","registered":"2025-09-23T02:50:51Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-23T02:50:51Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16534","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16534","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.16534","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cheng","familyName":"Jiayang","name":"Jiayang, Cheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qianqian","familyName":"Zhuang","name":"Zhuang, Qianqian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoran","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Haoran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chunkit","familyName":"Chan","name":"Chan, Chunkit","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xin","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Xin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lin","familyName":"Qiu","name":"Qiu, Lin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yangqiu","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Yangqiu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"InteGround: On the Evaluation of Verification and Retrieval Planning in Integrative Grounding"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-20T04:48:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T00:17:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Grounding large language models (LLMs) in external knowledge sources is a promising method for faithful prediction. While existing grounding approaches work well for simple queries, many real-world information needs require synthesizing multiple pieces of evidence. We introduce \"integrative grounding\" -- the challenge of retrieving and verifying multiple inter-dependent pieces of evidence to support a hypothesis query. To systematically study this problem, we repurpose data from four domains for evaluating integrative grounding capabilities. Our investigation reveals two critical findings: First, in groundedness verification, while LLMs are robust to redundant evidence, they tend to rationalize using internal knowledge when information is incomplete. Second, in examining retrieval planning strategies, we find that undirected planning can degrade performance through noise introduction, while premise abduction emerges as a promising approach due to its logical constraints. Additionally, LLMs' zero-shot self-reflection capabilities consistently improve grounding quality. These insights provide valuable direction for developing more effective integrative grounding systems."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to EMNLP 2025 Findings"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.16534","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-23T02:50:40Z","registered":"2025-09-23T02:50:40Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-23T02:50:40Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16533","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16533","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.16533","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sungwon","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Sungwon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daniel","familyName":"Khashabi","name":"Khashabi, Daniel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Challenging the Evaluator: LLM Sycophancy Under User Rebuttal"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-20T04:44:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T00:17:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) often exhibit sycophancy, distorting responses to align with user beliefs, notably by readily agreeing with user counterarguments. Paradoxically, LLMs are increasingly adopted as successful evaluative agents for tasks such as grading and adjudicating claims. This research investigates that tension: why do LLMs show sycophancy when challenged in subsequent conversational turns, yet perform well when evaluating conflicting arguments presented simultaneously? We empirically tested these contrasting scenarios by varying key interaction patterns. We find that state-of-the-art models: (1) are more likely to endorse a user's counterargument when framed as a follow-up from a user, rather than when both responses are presented simultaneously for evaluation; (2) show increased susceptibility to persuasion when the user's rebuttal includes detailed reasoning, even when the conclusion of the reasoning is incorrect; and (3) are more readily swayed by casually phrased feedback than by formal critiques, even when the casual input lacks justification. Our results highlight the risk of relying on LLMs for judgment tasks without accounting for conversational framing."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to EMNLP 2025 Findings"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.16533","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-23T02:50:38Z","registered":"2025-09-23T02:50:39Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-23T02:50:39Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16530","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16530","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.16530","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuoyoucheng","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Shuoyoucheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhenhua","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zhenhua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Enze","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Enze","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kai","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Kai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaobing","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Xiaobing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Baosheng","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Baosheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"AIPsychoBench: Understanding the Psychometric Differences between LLMs and Humans"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-20T04:40:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T00:17:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) with hundreds of billions of parameters have exhibited human-like intelligence by learning from vast amounts of internet-scale data. However, the uninterpretability of large-scale neural networks raises concerns about the reliability of LLM. Studies have attempted to assess the psychometric properties of LLMs by borrowing concepts from human psychology to enhance their interpretability, but they fail to account for the fundamental differences between LLMs and humans. This results in high rejection rates when human scales are reused directly. Furthermore, these scales do not support the measurement of LLM psychological property variations in different languages. This paper introduces AIPsychoBench, a specialized benchmark tailored to assess the psychological properties of LLM. It uses a lightweight role-playing prompt to bypass LLM alignment, improving the average effective response rate from 70.12% to 90.40%. Meanwhile, the average biases are only 3.3% (positive) and 2.1% (negative), which are significantly lower than the biases of 9.8% and 6.9%, respectively, caused by traditional jailbreak prompts. Furthermore, among the total of 112 psychometric subcategories, the score deviations for seven languages compared to English ranged from 5% to 20.2% in 43 subcategories, providing the first comprehensive evidence of the linguistic impact on the psychometrics of LLM."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Thank you for your attention. This paper was accepted by the CogSci 2025 conference in April and published in August. The location in the proceedings is: https://escholarship.org/uc/item/39k8f46q"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.16530","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-23T02:50:34Z","registered":"2025-09-23T02:50:35Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-23T02:50:35Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16521","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16521","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.16521","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yifan","familyName":"Yan","name":"Yan, Yifan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuai","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Shuai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiuzhen","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Xiuzhen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiangguang","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Xiangguang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Chow","name":"Chow, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuanchao","familyName":"Shu","name":"Shu, Yuanchao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shibo","familyName":"He","name":"He, Shibo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"mmExpert: Integrating Large Language Models for Comprehensive mmWave Data Synthesis and Understanding"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-20T04:01:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T00:16:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Millimeter-wave (mmWave) sensing technology holds significant value in human-centric applications, yet the high costs associated with data acquisition and annotation limit its widespread adoption in our daily lives. Concurrently, the rapid evolution of large language models (LLMs) has opened up opportunities for addressing complex human needs. This paper presents mmExpert, an innovative mmWave understanding framework consisting of a data generation flywheel that leverages LLMs to automate the generation of synthetic mmWave radar datasets for specific application scenarios, thereby training models capable of zero-shot generalization in real-world environments. Extensive experiments demonstrate that the data synthesized by mmExpert significantly enhances the performance of downstream models and facilitates the successful deployment of large models for mmWave understanding."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to ACM MobiHoc '25"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.16521","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-23T02:50:21Z","registered":"2025-09-23T02:50:22Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-23T02:50:22Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16519","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16519","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.16519","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yang","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"PM25Vision: A Large-Scale Benchmark Dataset for Visual Estimation of Air Quality"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-20T03:51:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T00:16:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We introduce PM25Vision (PM25V), the largest and most comprehensive dataset to date for estimating air quality - specifically PM2.5 concentrations - from street-level images. The dataset contains over 11,114 images matched with timestamped and geolocated PM2.5 readings across 3,261 AQI monitoring stations and 11 years, significantly exceeding the scale of previous benchmarks. The spatial accuracy of this dataset has reached 5 kilometers, far exceeding the city-level accuracy of many datasets. We describe the data collection, synchronization, and cleaning pipelines, and provide baseline model performances using CNN and transformer architectures. Our dataset is publicly available."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.16519","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-23T02:50:18Z","registered":"2025-09-23T02:50:19Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-23T02:50:19Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16517","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16517","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.16517","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Burak","familyName":"Satar","name":"Satar, Burak","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhixin","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Zhixin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Patrick A.","familyName":"Irawan","name":"Irawan, Patrick A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wilfried A.","familyName":"Mulyawan","name":"Mulyawan, Wilfried A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jing","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Jing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ee-Peng","familyName":"Lim","name":"Lim, Ee-Peng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chong-Wah","familyName":"Ngo","name":"Ngo, Chong-Wah","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Seeing Culture: A Benchmark for Visual Reasoning and Grounding"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multimedia (cs.MM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-20T03:47:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T00:16:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multimodal vision-language models (VLMs) have made substantial progress in various tasks that require a combined understanding of visual and textual content, particularly in cultural understanding tasks, with the emergence of new cultural datasets. However, these datasets frequently fall short of providing cultural reasoning while underrepresenting many cultures. In this paper, we introduce the Seeing Culture Benchmark (SCB), focusing on cultural reasoning with a novel approach that requires VLMs to reason on culturally rich images in two stages: i) selecting the correct visual option with multiple-choice visual question answering (VQA), and ii) segmenting the relevant cultural artifact as evidence of reasoning. Visual options in the first stage are systematically organized into three types: those originating from the same country, those from different countries, or a mixed group. Notably, all options are derived from a singular category for each type. Progression to the second stage occurs only after a correct visual option is chosen. The SCB benchmark comprises 1,065 images that capture 138 cultural artifacts across five categories from seven Southeast Asia countries, whose diverse cultures are often overlooked, accompanied by 3,178 questions, of which 1,093 are unique and meticulously curated by human annotators. Our evaluation of various VLMs reveals the complexities involved in cross-modal cultural reasoning and highlights the disparity between visual reasoning and spatial grounding in culturally nuanced scenarios. The SCB serves as a crucial benchmark for identifying these shortcomings, thereby guiding future developments in the field of cultural reasoning. https://github.com/buraksatar/SeeingCulture"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to EMNLP 2025 Main Conference, https://seeingculture-benchmark.github.io/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.16517","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-23T02:50:16Z","registered":"2025-09-23T02:50:16Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-23T02:50:16Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16508","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16508","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.16508","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marijan","familyName":"Fofonjka","name":"Fofonjka, Marijan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shahryar","familyName":"Zehtabi","name":"Zehtabi, Shahryar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alireza","familyName":"Behtash","name":"Behtash, Alireza","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tyler","familyName":"Mauer","name":"Mauer, Tyler","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David","familyName":"Stout","name":"Stout, David","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Federated Learning with Ad-hoc Adapter Insertions: The Case of Soft-Embeddings for Training Classifier-as-Retriever"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-20T03:07:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T00:15:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"When existing retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) solutions are intended to be used for new knowledge domains, it is necessary to update their encoders, which are taken to be pretrained large language models (LLMs). However, fully finetuning these large models is compute- and memory-intensive, and even infeasible when deployed on resource-constrained edge devices. We propose a novel encoder architecture in this work that addresses this limitation by using a frozen small language model (SLM), which satisfies the memory constraints of edge devices, and inserting a small adapter network before the transformer blocks of the SLM. The trainable adapter takes the token embeddings of the new corpus and learns to produce enhanced soft embeddings for it, while requiring significantly less compute power to update than full fine-tuning. We further propose a novel retrieval mechanism by attaching a classifier head to the SLM encoder, which is trained to learn a similarity mapping of the input embeddings to their corresponding documents. Finally, to enable the online fine-tuning of both (i) the encoder soft embeddings and (ii) the classifier-as-retriever on edge devices, we adopt federated learning (FL) and differential privacy (DP) to achieve an efficient, privacy-preserving, and product-grade training solution. We conduct a theoretical analysis of our methodology, establishing convergence guarantees under mild assumptions on gradient variance when deployed for general smooth nonconvex loss functions. Through extensive numerical experiments, we demonstrate (i) the efficacy of obtaining soft embeddings to enhance the encoder, (ii) training a classifier to improve the retriever, and (iii) the role of FL in achieving speedup."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"22 pages, 7 figures, 3 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.16508","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-23T02:50:03Z","registered":"2025-09-23T02:50:04Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-23T02:50:04Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16507","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16507","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.16507","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hanting","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Hanting","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huaao","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Huaao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianhong","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Jianhong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianxiong","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Tianxiong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiulong","familyName":"Cui","name":"Cui, Jiulong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haizhen","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Haizhen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yan","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jie","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Jie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"OS-DiffVSR: Towards One-step Latent Diffusion Model for High-detailed Real-world Video Super-Resolution"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-20T03:04:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T00:15:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recently, latent diffusion models has demonstrated promising performance in real-world video super-resolution (VSR) task, which can reconstruct high-quality videos from distorted low-resolution input through multiple diffusion steps. Compared to image super-resolution (ISR), VSR methods needs to process each frame in a video, which poses challenges to its inference efficiency. However, video quality and inference efficiency have always been a trade-off for the diffusion-based VSR methods. In this work, we propose One-Step Diffusion model for real-world Video Super-Resolution, namely OS-DiffVSR. Specifically, we devise a novel adjacent frame adversarial training paradigm, which can significantly improve the quality of synthetic videos. Besides, we devise a multi-frame fusion mechanism to maintain inter-frame temporal consistency and reduce the flicker in video. Extensive experiments on several popular VSR benchmarks demonstrate that OS-DiffVSR can even achieve better quality than existing diffusion-based VSR methods that require dozens of sampling steps."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.16507","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-23T02:50:02Z","registered":"2025-09-23T02:50:02Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-23T02:50:02Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16502","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16502","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.16502","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jialin","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Jialin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Houyu","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Houyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Seongjun","familyName":"Yun","name":"Yun, Seongjun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alejandro","familyName":"Mottini","name":"Mottini, Alejandro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rex","familyName":"Ying","name":"Ying, Rex","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiang","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Xiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vassilis N.","familyName":"Ioannidis","name":"Ioannidis, Vassilis N.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zheng","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Zheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qingjun","familyName":"Cui","name":"Cui, Qingjun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"GRIL: Knowledge Graph Retrieval-Integrated Learning with Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-20T02:38:00Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T00:14:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) has significantly mitigated the hallucinations of Large Language Models (LLMs) by grounding the generation with external knowledge. Recent extensions of RAG to graph-based retrieval offer a promising direction, leveraging the structural knowledge for multi-hop reasoning. However, existing graph RAG typically decouples retrieval and reasoning processes, which prevents the retriever from adapting to the reasoning needs of the LLM. They also struggle with scalability when performing multi-hop expansion over large-scale graphs, or depend heavily on annotated ground-truth entities, which are often unavailable in open-domain settings. To address these challenges, we propose a novel graph retriever trained end-to-end with LLM, which features an attention-based growing and pruning mechanism, adaptively navigating multi-hop relevant entities while filtering out noise. Within the extracted subgraph, structural knowledge and semantic features are encoded via soft tokens and the verbalized graph, respectively, which are infused into the LLM together, thereby enhancing its reasoning capability and facilitating interactive joint training of the graph retriever and the LLM reasoner. Experimental results across three QA benchmarks show that our approach consistently achieves state-of-the-art performance, validating the strength of joint graph-LLM optimization for complex reasoning tasks. Notably, our framework eliminates the need for predefined ground-truth entities by directly optimizing the retriever using LLM logits as implicit feedback, making it especially effective in open-domain settings."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.16502","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-23T02:49:55Z","registered":"2025-09-23T02:49:55Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-23T02:49:55Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16491","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16491","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.16491","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lovely Yeswanth","familyName":"Panchumarthi","name":"Panchumarthi, Lovely Yeswanth","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Saurabh","familyName":"Kataria","name":"Kataria, Saurabh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yi","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Yi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiao","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Xiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alex","familyName":"Fedorov","name":"Fedorov, Alex","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hyunjung Gloria","familyName":"Kwak","name":"Kwak, Hyunjung Gloria","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"FairTune: A Bias-Aware Fine-Tuning Framework Towards Fair Heart Rate Prediction from PPG"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computational Engineering, Finance, and Science (cs.CE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-20T01:42:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T00:13:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Foundation models pretrained on physiological data such as photoplethysmography (PPG) signals are increasingly used to improve heart rate (HR) prediction across diverse settings. Fine-tuning these models for local deployment is often seen as a practical and scalable strategy. However, its impact on demographic fairness particularly under domain shifts remains underexplored. We fine-tune PPG-GPT a transformer-based foundation model pretrained on intensive care unit (ICU) data across three heterogeneous datasets (ICU, wearable, smartphone) and systematically evaluate the effects on HR prediction accuracy and gender fairness. While fine-tuning substantially reduces mean absolute error (up to 80%), it can simultaneously widen fairness gaps, especially in larger models and under significant distributional characteristics shifts. To address this, we introduce FairTune, a bias-aware fine-tuning framework in which we benchmark three mitigation strategies: class weighting based on inverse group frequency (IF), Group Distributionally Robust Optimization (GroupDRO), and adversarial debiasing (ADV). We find that IF and GroupDRO significantly reduce fairness gaps without compromising accuracy, with effectiveness varying by deployment domain. Representation analyses further reveal that mitigation techniques reshape internal embeddings to reduce demographic clustering. Our findings highlight that fairness does not emerge as a natural byproduct of fine-tuning and that explicit mitigation is essential for equitable deployment of physiological foundation models."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.16491","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-23T02:49:39Z","registered":"2025-09-23T02:49:40Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-23T02:49:40Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}}],"meta":{"total":440,"totalPages":18,"page":16},"links":{"self":"https://api.datacite.org/dois?client-id=arxiv.content\u0026page%5Bnumber%5D=16\u0026page%5Bsize%5D=25\u0026query=registered%3A%5B20